    shortDescription: "Compare encryption methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSymmetric encryption uses the same key for encryption and decryption (fast, but key distribution is hard). Asymmetric encryption uses a public/private key pair (slower, but enables secure key exchange and digital signatures).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between symmetric and asymmetric encryption and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Symmetric encryption uses the same key for encryption and decryption (fast, but key distribution is hard).\n2. Asymmetric encryption uses a public/private key pair (slower, but enables secure key exchange and digital signatures).\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Symmetric encryption uses the same key for encryption and decryption (fast, but key distribution is hard).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Symmetric encryption uses the same key for encryption and decryption (fast, but key distribution is hard).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
