    shortDescription: "Clarify the terms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA reverse proxy is a server that forwards client requests to backend servers. An API gateway is a type of reverse proxy that also provides additional features like authentication, rate limiting, request transformation, and service discovery.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a gateway and a reverse proxy and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A reverse proxy is a server that forwards client requests to backend servers.\n2. An API gateway is a type of reverse proxy that also provides additional features like authentication, rate limiting, request transformation, and service discovery.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A reverse proxy is a server that forwards client requests to backend servers.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A reverse proxy is a server that forwards client requests to backend servers.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
