    slug: "set-slo",
    shortDescription: "Explain the process.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn SLO is a target for service performance (e.g., 99.9% availability). Set it based on user expectations, business requirements, and historical performance. Use error budgets to balance reliability and feature development.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a service level objective (SLO) and how do you set it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An SLO is a target for service performance (e.g., 99.9% availability).\n2. Set it based on user expectations, business requirements, and historical performance.\n3. Use error budgets to balance reliability and feature development.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. An SLO is a target for service performance (e.g., 99.9% availability).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An SLO is a target for service performance (e.g., 99.9% availability).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
